\DOC X86_UNDEFINED_GEN_TAC

\TYPE {X86_UNDEFINED_GEN_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Strips the outermost universal quantifier of the goal, instantiating the bound
variable with a freshly generated variable named {undefined_<n>}, where {n} is
a counter that advances past the numbers already occurring in the goal.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes no arguments. Applied to a goal whose conclusion is
{!x1. t[x1]}, it removes that single quantifier and replaces {x1} throughout
the body by a new variable of the same type:
{
   A ?- !x1. t[x1]
   ================== X86_UNDEFINED_GEN_TAC
   A ?- t[undefined_n]
}
\noindent Only the outermost universal quantifier is consumed; any further
universal quantifiers remain in place, as does any inner existential
quantification ({?y1 ... yn.}) under which the fresh variable may land. In the
observed traces the quantified body is an implication whose antecedent equates
a state-update term with a state variable, and the fresh variable appears
inside that term.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this one kind of transformation, always on goals
whose conclusion begins with a universal quantifier.

\ENDDOC
